<resources>
    <!--
      DayNight, so the window the system paints before Compose draws matches the
      theme Compose is about to apply. A light window flashing behind a dark app
      is the same class of mistake as the missing Surface: nothing is wrong with
      the theme, it just is not being applied to everything.
    -->
    <!--
      DeviceDefault, not DeviceDefault.DayNight: the DayNight variant arrived
      in API 29 and this app runs from 26, where asking for it fails to inflate
      the theme and the app dies on launch. Nothing is lost below 29 — that is
      the release where a system-wide dark setting exists to follow at all. The
      DayNight parent is in values-v29, where it means something.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Ducat" parent="android:Theme.DeviceDefault">
        <item name="android:windowActionBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowNoTitle">true</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The launch theme. The system shows this from the moment the process starts,
      which is the window a Compose splash cannot cover: by the time Compose can
      draw, the wait it was meant to hide is over.

      Held only until the first frame — Veilid takes seconds to attach and the
      app is usable long before that, so blocking on the node would turn a
      splash into a delay.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Ducat.Splash" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/ic_launcher_background</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/avd_splash</item>
        <!-- Must match the drawable, or the system cuts it off at its own
             default and the D never lands. -->
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimationDuration">800</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/Theme.Ducat</item>
    </style>
</resources>
